  "response": "**Setting up Bayes' theorem:**\n\n- Prevalence: P(disease) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(no disease) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|disease) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(–|no disease) = 0.95, so P(+|no disease) = 0.05 (false positive rate)\n\n**Calculation:**\n\nP(disease | +) = [P(+|disease)·P(disease)] / [P(+|disease)·P(disease) + P(+|no disease)·P(no disease)]\n\nNumerator: 0.98 × 0.002 = 0.00196\n\nDenominator: 0.00196 + (0.05 × 0.998) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\nP(disease | +) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794 = 3.7794%\n\nEven with a fairly sensitive and specific test, because the condition is rare (0.2% prevalence), most positive results are still false positives — only about 1 in 26 people who test positive actually have the condition.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
